				<h1 class=3D"entry-title" itemprop=3D"headline">Arduino IDE 2: Install =
ESP32 LittleFS Uploader (Upload Files to the Filesystem)</h1>			</header>
		=09
		<div class=3D"entry-content" itemprop=3D"text">
		=09
<p>In this guide, you=E2=80=99ll learn how to upload files to the ESP32 Lit=
tleFS Filesystem using Arduino IDE 2 (2.2.1 or a higher version). We=E2=80=
=99ll install an uploader plugin that will add a new menu to the Arduino ID=
E. This plugin is also compatible with the ESP8266 and Raspberry Pi Pico bo=
ards.</p>


<div class=3D"wp-block-image">
<figure class=3D"aligncenter size-full"><a href=3D"https://i0.wp.com/random=
nerdtutorials.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/Installing-LittleFS-Plugin-Ard=
uino-IDE-2-ESP32.jpg?quality=3D100&amp;strip=3Dall&amp;ssl=3D1"><img data-r=
ecalc-dims=3D"1" fetchpriority=3D"high" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"828" he=
ight=3D"466" src=3D"https://i0.wp.com/randomnerdtutorials.com/wp-content/up=
loads/2024/05/Installing-LittleFS-Plugin-Arduino-IDE-2-ESP32.jpg?resize=3D8=
28%2C466&amp;quality=3D100&amp;strip=3Dall&amp;ssl=3D1" alt=3D"Arduino IDE =
2 Install ESP32 LittleFS Uploader Upload Files to the Filesystem" class=3D"=
wp-image-154916" loading=3D"eager"></a></figure></div>


<p class=3D"rntbox rntclblue"><strong>Using an ESP8266?</strong> Follow thi=
s tutorial instead: <a href=3D"https://randomnerdtutorials.com/arduino-ide-=
2-install-esp8266-littlefs/">Arduino IDE 2: Install ESP8266 NodeMCU LittleF=
S Uploader (Upload Files to the Filesystem)</a>.</p>



<p><strong>Table of Contents</strong></p>



<ul class=3D"wp-block-list">
<li><a href=3D"https://randomnerdtutorials.com/arduino-ide-2-install-esp32-=
littlefs/#introduction">Introducing LittleFS</a></li>



<li><a href=3D"https://randomnerdtutorials.com/arduino-ide-2-install-esp32-=
littlefs/#installing">Installing LittleFS Filesystem Uploader Plugin</a>
<ul class=3D"wp-block-list">
<li><a href=3D"https://randomnerdtutorials.com/arduino-ide-2-install-esp32-=
littlefs/#installing-windows">Windows Instructions</a></li>



<li><a href=3D"https://randomnerdtutorials.com/arduino-ide-2-install-esp32-=
littlefs/#installing-MAC">Mac OS X Instructions</a></li>
</ul>
</li>



<li><a href=3D"https://randomnerdtutorials.com/arduino-ide-2-install-esp32-=
littlefs/#upload-files">Uploading Files to ESP32 using the Filesystem Uploa=
der</a></li>



<li><a href=3D"https://randomnerdtutorials.com/arduino-ide-2-install-esp32-=
littlefs/#testing">Testing the ESP32 LittleFS Uploader</a></li>
</ul>



<p class=3D"rntbox rntclgray">If you=E2=80=99re still using <strong>Arduino=
 1.8</strong>, you can follow this tutorial instead: <a href=3D"https://ran=
domnerdtutorials.com/esp32-littlefs-arduino-ide/">ESP32: Upload Files to Li=
ttleFS using Arduino IDE (legacy)</a>.</p>



<h2 class=3D"wp-block-heading" id=3D"introduction">Introducing LittleFS</h2=
>



<p>LittleFS is a lightweight filesystem created for microcontrollers that l=
ets you access the flash memory as you do in a standard file system on your=
 computer, but it=E2=80=99s simpler and more limited. You can read, write, =
close, and delete files. Using LittleFS with the ESP32 boards is useful to:=
</p>



<ul class=3D"wp-block-list">
<li>Create configuration files with settings;</li>



<li>Save data permanently;</li>



<li>Create files to save small amounts of data instead of using a microSD c=
ard;</li>



<li>Save HTML, CSS, and JavaScript files to build a web server;</li>



<li><a href=3D"https://randomnerdtutorials.com/display-images-esp32-esp8266=
-web-server/">Save images, figures, and icons</a>;</li>



<li>And much more.</li>
</ul>



<h2 class=3D"wp-block-heading" id=3D"installing">Installing the LittleFS Up=
loader Plugin on Arduino IDE 2</h2>



<p>To upload files to the ESP32 on <a href=3D"https://randomnerdtutorials.c=
om/installing-esp32-arduino-ide-2-0/" title=3D"">Arduino IDE 2</a>, we=E2=
=80=99ll use <a href=3D"https://github.com/earlephilhower/arduino-littlefs-=
upload" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener" title=3D"">this LittleFS Uploade=
r plugin</a> that is compatible with Arduino 2.2.1 or higher and can be use=
d with the ESP32, <a href=3D"https://randomnerdtutorials.com/arduino-ide-2-=
install-esp8266-littlefs/" title=3D"">ESP8266</a>,<a href=3D"https://random=
nerdtutorials.com/projects-esp8266/" title=3D""> </a>and <a href=3D"https:/=
/randomnerdtutorials.com/projects-raspberry-pi-pico/" title=3D"">Raspberry =
Pi Pico boards</a>.</p>



<h3 class=3D"wp-block-heading" id=3D"installing-windows">Windows Instructio=
ns</h3>



<p>Follow the next steps to install the filesystem uploader if you=E2=80=99=
re using Windows (<a href=3D"https://randomnerdtutorials.com/arduino-ide-2-=
install-esp32-littlefs/#installing-MAC" title=3D"">click here for MacOS ins=
tructions</a>):</p>



<p><strong>1)</strong> Go to the <a href=3D"https://github.com/earlephilhow=
er/arduino-littlefs-upload/releases" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener" tit=
le=3D"">releases page</a> and click the <em>.vsix</em> file to download.</p=
>


<div class=3D"wp-block-image">
<figure class=3D"aligncenter size-full"><img data-recalc-dims=3D"1" decodin=
g=3D"async" width=3D"750" height=3D"446" src=3D"https://i0.wp.com/randomner=
dtutorials.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/arduino-littlefs-upload-vsix-file=
.png?resize=3D750%2C446&amp;quality=3D100&amp;strip=3Dall&amp;ssl=3D1" alt=
=3D"Download vsix file to install filesystem uploader plugin" class=3D"wp-i=
mage-154908" loading=3D"eager" fetchpriority=3D"high"></figure></div>


<p><strong>2)</strong> On your computer, go to the following path: <span cl=
ass=3D"rnthl rntliteral">C:\Users\&lt;username&gt;\.arduinoIDE\</span>. Cre=
ate a new folder called <em>plugins </em>if you haven=E2=80=99t already.</p=
>


<div class=3D"wp-block-image">
<figure class=3D"aligncenter size-full"><img data-recalc-dims=3D"1" decodin=
g=3D"async" width=3D"828" height=3D"482" src=3D"https://i0.wp.com/randomner=
dtutorials.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/create-plugins-folder-arduino-ide=
.png?resize=3D828%2C482&amp;quality=3D100&amp;strip=3Dall&amp;ssl=3D1" alt=
=3D"Create Plugins Folder Arduino IDE 2" class=3D"wp-image-148535" loading=
=3D"eager" fetchpriority=3D"high"></figure></div>


<p><strong>3)</strong> Move the <em>.vsix</em> file you downloaded previous=
ly to the <em>plugins </em>folder (remove any other previous versions of th=
e same plugin if that=E2=80=99s the case).</p>


<div class=3D"wp-block-image">
<figure class=3D"aligncenter size-full"><img data-recalc-dims=3D"1" loading=
=3D"eager" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"777" height=3D"271" src=3D"https://i=
0.wp.com/randomnerdtutorials.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/arduino-ide-2-p=
lugins.png?resize=3D777%2C271&amp;quality=3D100&amp;strip=3Dall&amp;ssl=3D1=
" alt=3D"Moving LittleFS uploader to the plugins folder arduino ide 2" clas=
s=3D"wp-image-154909" fetchpriority=3D"high"></figure></div>


<p><strong>4)</strong> Restart or open the Arduino IDE 2. To check if the p=
lugin was successfully installed, press [<strong>Ctrl</strong>] + [<strong>=
Shift</strong>] + [<strong>P</strong>] to open the command palette. An inst=
ruction called =E2=80=98<strong>Upload Little FS to Pico/ESP8266/ESP32</str=
ong>=E2=80=98 should be there (just scroll down or search for the name of t=
he instruction).</p>


<div class=3D"wp-block-image">
<figure class=3D"aligncenter size-full"><img data-recalc-dims=3D"1" loading=
=3D"lazy" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"732" height=3D"586" src=3D"https://i0=
.wp.com/randomnerdtutorials.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/Upload-littlefs-=
esp32-esp8266-pico.png?resize=3D732%2C586&amp;quality=3D100&amp;strip=3Dall=
&amp;ssl=3D1" alt=3D"Upload LittleFS to Pico/ESP8266/ESP32" class=3D"wp-ima=
ge-154910" fetchpriority=3D"low"></figure></div>


<p>That means the plugin was installed successfully. Proceed to <a href=3D"=
https://randomnerdtutorials.com/arduino-ide-2-install-esp32-littlefs/#uploa=
d-files" title=3D"">this section </a>to test the filesystem uploader plugin=
.</p>



<h3 class=3D"wp-block-heading" id=3D"installing-MAC">Mac OS X Instructions<=
/h3>



<p>Follow the next steps to install the filesystem uploader if you=E2=80=99=
re using Mac OS X:</p>



<p><strong>1)</strong> Go to the <a href=3D"https://github.com/earlephilhow=
er/arduino-littlefs-upload/releases" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener" tit=
le=3D"">releases page</a> and click the <em>.vsix</em> file to download.</p=
>


<div class=3D"wp-block-image">
<figure class=3D"aligncenter size-full"><img data-recalc-dims=3D"1" loading=
=3D"lazy" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"750" height=3D"446" src=3D"https://i0=
.wp.com/randomnerdtutorials.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/arduino-littlefs=
-upload-vsix-file.png?resize=3D750%2C446&amp;quality=3D100&amp;strip=3Dall&=
amp;ssl=3D1" alt=3D"LittleFS Uploader Plugin ESP32 ESP8266 Pico Upload File=
s to the Filesystem" class=3D"wp-image-154908" fetchpriority=3D"low"></figu=
re></div>


<p><strong>2)</strong> In Finder, type <span class=3D"rnthl rntliteral">~/.=
arduinoIDE/</span> and open that directory.</p>


<div class=3D"wp-block-image">
<figure class=3D"aligncenter size-full"><img data-recalc-dims=3D"1" loading=
=3D"lazy" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"750" height=3D"203" src=3D"https://i0=
.wp.com/randomnerdtutorials.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/Arduino-IDE-Dire=
ctory-MACOS-f.png?resize=3D750%2C203&amp;quality=3D100&amp;strip=3Dall&amp;=
ssl=3D1" alt=3D"In Finder type ~/.arduinoIDE open that directory" class=3D"=
wp-image-148588" fetchpriority=3D"low"></figure></div>


<p><strong>3)</strong> Create a new folder called <em>plugins</em>.</p>


<div class=3D"wp-block-image">
<figure class=3D"aligncenter size-full"><img data-recalc-dims=3D"1" loading=
=3D"lazy" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"750" height=3D"316" src=3D"https://i0=
.wp.com/randomnerdtutorials.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/Create-plugins-f=
older-arduino-ide-macos.png?resize=3D750%2C316&amp;quality=3D100&amp;strip=
=3Dall&amp;ssl=3D1" alt=3D"creating a plugins folder Arduino IDE 2 MacOS" c=
lass=3D"wp-image-148555" fetchpriority=3D"low"></figure></div>


<p><strong>4)</strong> Move the <em>.vsix</em> file to the <em>plugins </em=
>folder (remove any other previous versions of the same plugin if that=E2=
=80=99s the case).</p>


<div class=3D"wp-block-image">
<figure class=3D"aligncenter size-full"><img data-recalc-dims=3D"1" loading=
=3D"lazy" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"750" height=3D"309" src=3D"https://i0=
.wp.com/randomnerdtutorials.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/moving-vsix-file=
-arduino-ide-2-mac-os.png?resize=3D750%2C309&amp;quality=3D100&amp;strip=3D=
all&amp;ssl=3D1" alt=3D"Moving vsix file Arduino IDE 2 in Mac OS - Uploader=
 Plugin" class=3D"wp-image-154928" fetchpriority=3D"low"></figure></div>


<p><strong>5)</strong> Restart or open the Arduino IDE 2. To check if the p=
lugin was successfully installed, press [<strong>=E2=8C=98</strong>] + [<st=
rong>Shift</strong>] + [<strong>P</strong>] to open the command palette. An=
 instruction called =E2=80=98<strong>Upload LittleFS to Pico/ESP8266/ESP32<=
/strong>=E2=80=98 should be there (just scroll down or search for the name =
of the instruction).</p>


<div class=3D"wp-block-image">
<figure class=3D"aligncenter size-full"><img data-recalc-dims=3D"1" loading=
=3D"lazy" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"750" height=3D"254" src=3D"https://i0=
.wp.com/randomnerdtutorials.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/Arduino-IDE-Uplo=
ad-Filesystem-Image-ESP32-MacOS.png?resize=3D750%2C254&amp;quality=3D100&am=
p;strip=3Dall&amp;ssl=3D1" alt=3D"Arduino IDE 2 Upload Filesystem Image ESP=
32" class=3D"wp-image-154931" fetchpriority=3D"low"></figure></div>


<h2 class=3D"wp-block-heading" id=3D"upload-files">Uploading Files to ESP32=
 using the Filesystem Uploader in Arduino IDE 2</h2>



<p>To upload files to the ESP32 LittleFS filesystem, follow the next instru=
ctions.</p>



<p><strong>1)</strong> Create an Arduino sketch and save it. For demonstrat=
ion purposes, you can save an empty sketch.</p>



<p><strong>2)</strong> Then, open the sketch folder. You can go to <strong>=
Sketch</strong> &gt; <strong>Show Sketch Folder</strong>. The folder where =
your sketch is saved should open.</p>


<div class=3D"wp-block-image">
<figure class=3D"aligncenter size-full"><img data-recalc-dims=3D"1" loading=
=3D"lazy" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"715" height=3D"374" src=3D"https://i0=
.wp.com/randomnerdtutorials.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/arduino-ide-2-sh=
ow-sketch-folder.png?resize=3D715%2C374&amp;quality=3D100&amp;strip=3Dall&a=
mp;ssl=3D1" alt=3D"Arduino IDE 2 Show Sketch Folder" class=3D"wp-image-1485=
41" fetchpriority=3D"low"></figure></div>


<p><strong>3)</strong> Inside that folder, create a new folder called <span=
 class=3D"rnthl rntliteral"><strong><em>data</em></strong></span>.</p>


<div class=3D"wp-block-image">
<figure class=3D"aligncenter size-full"><img data-recalc-dims=3D"1" loading=
=3D"lazy" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"250" height=3D"129" src=3D"https://i0=
.wp.com/randomnerdtutorials.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/creating-data-fo=
lder.png?resize=3D250%2C129&amp;quality=3D100&amp;strip=3Dall&amp;ssl=3D1" =
alt=3D"Creating a data folder inside the sketch folder" class=3D"wp-image-1=
48542" fetchpriority=3D"low"></figure></div>


<p><strong>4)</strong> Inside the <span class=3D"rnthl rntliteral"><strong>=
<em>data</em></strong></span> folder is where you should put the files you =
want to upload to the ESP32 filesystem. As an example, create a <em>.txt</e=
m> file with some text called <em>test_example</em>.<em>txt</em> (and save =
it inside the <em>data </em>folder).</p>


<div class=3D"wp-block-image">
<figure class=3D"aligncenter size-full"><img data-recalc-dims=3D"1" loading=
=3D"lazy" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"515" height=3D"265" src=3D"https://i0=
.wp.com/randomnerdtutorials.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/test_file_esp32_=
filesystem.png?resize=3D515%2C265&amp;quality=3D100&amp;strip=3Dall&amp;ssl=
=3D1" alt=3D"Testing the ESP32 filesystem txt file" class=3D"wp-image-15491=
2" fetchpriority=3D"low"></figure></div>


<p><strong>5)</strong> Make sure you have the right board (<strong>Tools </=
strong>&gt; <strong>Board</strong>) and COM port selected (<strong>Tools </=
strong>&gt; <strong>Port</strong>).</p>



<p><strong>6)</strong> Depending on the ESP32 board selected, you may need =
to select the desired flash size (some boards don=E2=80=99t have that optio=
n, don=E2=80=99t worry). In the Arduino IDE, in <strong>Tools</strong> &gt;=
 <strong>Flash size</strong>, select the desired flash size (this will depe=
nd on the size of your files).</p>



<p><strong>7)</strong> Then, upload the files to the ESP32 board. Press [<s=
trong>Ctrl</strong>] + [<strong>Shift</strong>] + [<strong>P</strong>] on W=
indows or [<strong>=E2=8C=98</strong>] + [<strong>Shift</strong>] + [<stron=
g>P</strong>] on MacOS to open the command palette. Search for the <strong>=
Upload LittleFS to Pico/ESP8266/ESP32</strong> command and click on it.</p>


